<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yonezawa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yonezawa is a city in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-prefecture">Yamagata Prefecture</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The Date clan made it their seat in 1548, and from 1601 it was the castle town of the Uesugi clan, whose lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-harunori">Uesugi Harunori</a> (Yōzan) reformed the domain's finances in the 18th century. The city is known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> weaving and for Yonezawa beef.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yonezawa.jpg" alt="Yonezawa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YonezawaCityMountainView.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yonezawa lies in the southeast corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-prefecture">Yamagata Prefecture</a> and covers 548.51 square kilometres. Its southern and eastern parts are river basins surrounded by large mountains, forming the Yonezawa Basin, and the Mogami River flows through the city. The south has complex terrain of several rivers and alternating ridges and valleys running east to west. Part of the city lies within the Bandai-Asahi National Park. The climate is humid continental, with warm to hot summers, cold winters and heavy precipitation from August to October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was part of ancient Dewa Province and was controlled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> by the Date clan. The warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> was born in Yonezawa. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the area became Yonezawa Domain under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, ruled by the Uesugi clan. After the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji period</a> it was organized into Minamiokitama District, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-prefecture">Yamagata Prefecture</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Yonezawa is best known for its local delicacies, among them apples, Yonezawa beef and carp, and as a castle town that was once home to the Uesugi clan, including the daimyō Uesugi Yozan. Its economy rests on agriculture, horticulture, light manufacturing and tourism. Local attractions include Yonezawa Castle, Tateyama Castle, the Yonezawa City Uesugi Museum, Namegawa Great Falls and the hot springs of Shirabu Onsen and Yonezawa Onsen. Yamagata University has a campus in the city. Natives include the architect Itō Chūta, the statesman Hirata Tosuke and the admirals Yamashita Gentarō and Chūichi Nagumo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yonezawa, Yamagata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Uesugi were moved to <b>Yonezawa</b> in 1601, Aizu passed to Gamo Hideyuki and then to Kato Yoshiaki.</blockquote><a href="/state/aizu-domain">Aizu Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Date Tanemune issued the detailed Jinkai-shu law code in 1536, and from 1542 a war with his son Harumune split the clan; Harumune won and in 1548 moved the family seat to <b>Yonezawa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/date-clan-domain">Date clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Sendai and <b>Yonezawa</b> sought pardon for them, and when this was refused, delegates met at Shiroishi in May 1868 and formed an alliance, joined by Nagaoka and other Echigo domains.</blockquote><a href="/state/ouetsu-reppan-domei">Ouetsu Reppan Domei</a></li><li><blockquote>Uesugi Kagekatsu opposed Tokugawa Ieyasu in 1600 and in 1601 was stripped of his vast Aizu fief, keeping only <b>Yonezawa</b> with 300,000 koku.</blockquote><a href="/state/yonezawa-domain">Yonezawa Domain</a></li></ul></section>
